+++++++++++From 467589f6b155a83b03e17668651bf1b8c4b98179 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++++++++++From: Doug Gabehart <dgabehar@gmail.com>
+++++++++++Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 20:53:05 -0500
+++++++++++Subject: [PATCH] grammar: translate PCRE shorthand escapes (\d \w \s) to GBNF
+++++++++++ classes
+++++++++++
+++++++++++Re-port (session ab59c6f1, 2026-09-02): the LLAMACPP_REF bump from
+++++++++++1464c62d88f699ec9700c8010bbfdbc603a9efd6 to
+++++++++++0f3a71be15af836d277c9f918adfafb45732677e broke this patch's original
+++++++++++`git apply` -- ggml-org/llama.cpp substantially refactored
+++++++++++common/json-schema-to-grammar.cpp between those two pins (error handling
+++++++++++moved from an accumulated `_errors`/`_warnings` vector with `return ""` to
+++++++++++a real C++ exception hierarchy -- `unsupported_pattern` for "valid regex,
+++++++++++no GBNF equivalent, soft-fail to accept-any-string" vs. `invalid_pattern`
+++++++++++for "malformed regex, hard-fail the whole conversion" -- plus a new
+++++++++++`gbnf_escape_length()` helper replacing the old inline `\\` + 2-char-copy
+++++++++++escape handling). Confirmed upstream has NOT independently fixed the
+++++++++++underlying bug: the new commit's own test suite still has a
+++++++++++"regexp with unsupported shorthand" case asserting \w degrades to
+++++++++++accept-any-string.
+++++++++++
+++++++++++Re-implemented the same fix (PCRE-style \d/\D/\w/\W/\s/\S shorthand
+++++++++++translated to real GBNF character classes, both standalone and inlined
+++++++++++inside [...] where unambiguous) against the new exception-based control
+++++++++++flow.
+++++++++++
+++++++++++SEMANTIC MAPPING DECISION: the one case with no exact precedent in the
+++++++++++old code is \D/\W/\S mixed inside a [...] class (no single-range GBNF
+++++++++++equivalent). Deliberately mapped to invalid_pattern (hard conversion
+++++++++++failure, propagates to a thrown std::invalid_argument naming the pattern
+++++++++++and escape) rather than unsupported_pattern (which _visit_pattern() would
+++++++++++catch and silently downgrade to "accept any string"). A silent
+++++++++++accept-any-string fallback is the WRONG outcome for a schema-critical
+++++++++++field like a validated ID/format pattern -- it would silently accept
+++++++++++anything instead of surfacing the pattern-authoring mistake, undoing the
+++++++++++entire point of this fix (loud, actionable errors over silently-wrong
+++++++++++grammar behavior). All \d/\w/\s and standalone \D/\W/\S paths are
+++++++++++unambiguous exact translations either way and are unaffected by this
+++++++++++question.
+++++++++++
+++++++++++TEST FILE NOT PORTED: tests/test-json-schema-to-grammar.cpp is
+++++++++++deliberately NOT touched by this re-port -- the fleet's own build compiles
+++++++++++with -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF, so the test binary is never built or run by
+++++++++++that image, and hand-authoring new expected-GBNF-string test assertions
+++++++++++without the ability to actually compile and run them risked committing
+++++++++++plausible-looking but wrong "documentation" more misleading than no test
+++++++++++change at all. If LLAMA_BUILD_TESTS is ever turned on, port the test
+++++++++++additions to the new file structure (`nlohmann::ordered_json::parse` ->
+++++++++++`common_json::parse`) and update the existing "regexp with unsupported
+++++++++++shorthand" test (pattern "^[0-9]{3}\\w$"), which currently asserts the
+++++++++++pre-fix accept-any-string behavior for \w and will fail once this fix is
+++++++++++compiled and that suite is run.
+++++++++++
+++++++++++VERIFICATION PERFORMED (session ab59c6f1, no live CI GPU/model available
+++++++++++for this check): applied cleanly (`git apply --check` + `git apply`,
+++++++++++byte-identical result reproduced independently twice), and the resulting
+++++++++++common/json-schema-to-grammar.cpp was compiled standalone with
+++++++++++`g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -fsyntax-only` and again as a full `-c`
+++++++++++object-file compile against the real repo headers -- zero errors, zero
+++++++++++warnings either way. This confirms the ported code is syntactically and
+++++++++++type-correct; it does not replace the runtime grammar-parse verification
+++++++++++a LLAMACPP_REF bump requires (GPU offload smoke test + live-pod
+++++++++++tool-calling check), which happens after the fleet's image builds.
+++++++++++
+++++++++++Originally found via a real production incident: a PagerDuty
+++++++++++create_schedule MCP tool's leap-year-validated ISO-8601 date regex using
+++++++++++PCRE shorthand escapes produced invalid GBNF that llama-server's grammar
+++++++++++parser rejected at parse time, silently disabling grammar-constrained
+++++++++++decoding for the ENTIRE tool-calling request, not just calls to the
+++++++++++offending tool.
+++++++++++
+++++++++++No upstream PR tracks this -- original work, not a vendored port.
