++++@@ -0,0 +1,439 @@
+++++From 84b660c0d8ed1279465ce15fc0c2ce403e2cd921 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++++From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
+++++Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:16:26 -0500
+++++Subject: [PATCH] ggml-cpu: extend CPU backend device registry to N locality
+++++ domains
+++++
+++++llama.cpp's CPU backend has always registered exactly one device
+++++(ggml_backend_cpu_reg_get_device_count() hardcoded `return 1;`), even
+++++though multi-chiplet CPUs (e.g. AMD Strix Halo's two CCDs, this fleet's
+++++gabesrv10) have real L3-cache-sharing boundaries that already work as
+++++device boundaries for multi-GPU tensor-split. This is "Track A" from
+++++the CPU-offload research (Strix Split artifact,
+++++https://claude.ai/artifact/Mht44KMMnoWH8DbnVvUUYk): give the CPU
+++++backend more than one device in-process, split by real measured
+++++locality rather than paying RPC's TCP/full-graph-serialization tax for
+++++the same idea across processes -- llama.cpp's own maintainers already
+++++proposed this twice upstream (discussions #12303, #19102).
+++++
+++++ggml_backend_cpu_detect_locality_domains() groups cores by identical
+++++/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cache/index3/shared_cpu_list content.
+++++Any /sys read failure, a single shared list covering every core, or a
+++++non-Linux host all collapse to one neutral domain (all-false cpumask)
+++++-- the existing single-device behavior, unchanged. Overridable via
+++++GGML_CPU_LOCALITY_SYSFS_ROOT so tests/test-cpu-device-split.cpp (added
+++++in a later commit) can point detection at a fixture directory instead
+++++of live hardware. Detection is memoized (static, once per process) and
+++++runs the first time the device registry is queried, which on the
+++++server happens inside ggml_backend_load_all() -- before
+++++llama_numa_init() (tools/server/server.cpp:109-110), preserving that
+++++function's existing single-CPU-device assumption.
+++++
+++++ggml_backend_cpu_reg_get_device_count()/get_device() now return one
+++++device object per detected domain instead of asserting index==0
+++++against a single static context. Domain 0 keeps returning the plain
+++++ggml_backend_cpu_buffer_type() singleton; domain_index > 0 gets its
+++++own distinct buffer-type instance (reusing the same alloc/name/
+++++alignment/is_host behavior, exposed non-static in ggml-backend-impl.h/
+++++ggml-backend.cpp for exactly this reuse) so ggml_backend_sched's
+++++tensor-to-backend routing can tell domains apart -- every CPU device
+++++would otherwise report support for domain 0's buffer type only.
+++++ggml_backend_sched's own scheduling logic is already fully generic
+++++over N backends of any kind and needed no change.
+++++
+++++New public ggml_backend_cpu_device_get_locality_mask() lets callers
+++++(the startup calibration step, and this commit's own test) read back
+++++a CPU device's detected core mask.
+++++
+++++Verified via a standalone probe against the public ggml-backend.h API
+++++and against a synthetic 2-domain GGML_CPU_LOCALITY_SYSFS_ROOT fixture:
+++++real hardware in this sandbox (single L3 domain) reports exactly 1 CPU
+++++device as before; the mocked fixture reports 2, each independently
+++++constructible via ggml_backend_dev_init().
+++++
