++++@@ -0,0 +1,215 @@
+++++From 1d8ec4bc5af0f9c4bde49260d0e4cb6723b0f203 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++++From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
+++++Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:17:09 -0500
+++++Subject: [PATCH] llama-model: weighted per-layer CPU split across locality
+++++ domains
+++++
+++++Extends the existing per-layer device-assignment table
+++++(get_layer_buft_list/dev_layer) so that whenever llama_model_params
+++++carries more than one CPU device's calibration weight, CPU-resident
+++++layers are apportioned across those devices proportional to weight,
+++++using the same splits[]/std::upper_bound proportional-split logic
+++++already used for multi-GPU --tensor-split -- just computed from a live
+++++per-domain matmul measurement instead of a user-supplied ratio. On
+++++every current fleet node other than gabesrv10, cpu_split_weights is
+++++empty (single detected domain, calibration short-circuited in the
+++++previous commit) so this is byte-for-byte the existing single-CPU-
+++++device assignment path.
+++++
+++++Also wires each non-zero-index CPU domain's own distinct buffer type
+++++(from the registry extension, first commit) into the per-layer buft
+++++list, so ggml_backend_sched routes a layer's tensors to the domain its
+++++weight actually assigned it to instead of everything defaulting to
+++++domain 0's buffer type.
+++++
+++++Verified end-to-end against a mocked 2-domain GGML_CPU_LOCALITY_SYSFS_ROOT
+++++fixture with a live llama-server instance: a real layer (layer 3, with
+++++this build's synthetic test model) was assigned to the second detected
+++++CPU domain ("CPU1"), confirmed via the resolve_fused_ops log line
+++++(src/llama-context.cpp) that fires only when a layer's device actually
+++++differs from model.dev_layer()'s default -- direct proof the weighted
+++++split is functioning, not just compiling.
+++++
+++++Known caveat, not fixed here (see FLEET.md's new "CPU locality-domain
+++++split" entry): that same resolve_fused_ops check found that a
+++++cross-domain layer split can make Flash Attention's fused-op tensor
+++++resolve to a different device than the rest of its layer, and the
+++++existing device-mismatch fallback correctly, but silently, disables FA
+++++for that context rather than crashing. This is a real (if graceful)
+++++performance regression risk on any node that both enables --cpu-split
+++++auto and engages a real >1-domain split -- whether the fix is keeping a
+++++layer's fused ops deterministically co-located, or something else, is a
+++++design decision flagged for Doug rather than made unilaterally here.
+++++
+++++Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet
